7. Resolve the Down input conflict with one-way platforms

This is critical.

Alva currently uses a fresh Down press to drop through eligible one-way platforms.

But Super Jump requires holding Down while standing on a platform.

Without changing the input behavior, the fighter may drop through the platform before Jump can be pressed.

Super Jump must always take priority over dropping through a platform.

Implement deliberate input disambiguation.

Recommended control behavior

Use a quick Down tap-and-release as the explicit platform drop command:

* Quick Down tap and release, without Jump: drop through an eligible one-way platform.
* Hold Down, then press Jump: execute Super Jump from the platform.
* Hold Down without pressing Jump: remain grounded; do not automatically drop.
* Down + Jump in the same input sample: execute Super Jump rather than drop.
* Holding Down in the air while descending: continue Fast Fall.
* Explicit dropPressed requests from a training or scripted controller should retain their separate intended behavior where appropriate.

Use a short, clearly defined tap threshold if necessary to distinguish a tap from a hold. A long hold followed by release should not unexpectedly produce a delayed platform drop.

Preserve restrictions on dropping through:

* The main floor cannot be dropped through.
* Solid platforms cannot be dropped through.
* Platforms with dropThrough: false cannot be dropped through.
* Existing move, Shield, stun, and other action restrictions remain valid.

In particular, test Sol-Noir’s non-droppable water-tower deck and Asperitas’s moving clouds.

Do not resolve this conflict by simply disabling platform drops permanently.

Update instructions so players understand the new tap-versus-hold distinction.

